/** \page requirements-adoption Requirements for adoption

\section requirements What do we need to ensure for a broad adoption of @c Graph ?

The following requirements are mandatory if we ever want to get people more into
@c Graph.

\subsection kokkos-single-code-requirement Single source code @c Kokkos promise

We must ensure that libraries that want to support
both @c Graph and "regular" execution space modes
need to write a single version of their source code.

\subsection eti-requirement Heavily templated libraries resort to ETI

Heavily templated libraries tend to use Explicit Template Instantiation (ETI).
Supporting @c Graph mode should not add any burden to that.

\subsection no-change-requirement Support existing libraries as is

Libraries should actually not be required to rewrite anything to be usable in a graph.

\section transition-to-graph How would people adopt @c Graph ?

Given the requirements from @ref requirements, and given the following
code snippet, how would people use @c Graph ?

@code
template <typename Exec, typename...>
void axpby(const Exec& exec, ...)
{
    Kokkos::parallel_for(
        Kokkos::RangePolicy<Exec>(exec, ...),
        Functor{...}
    );
}
@endcode

\subsection adoption-rewrite-api Modify the API

A first solution would be to require libraries to modify their public API
to support @c Graph (chain of sender-like nodes).
It would typically require 2 changes:
1. The template type should allow for either an execution space type (*e.g.*
   @c Kokkos::Cuda) or the tail of sender-like chain of nodes.
2. The function must return the new sender, which would be the execution space instance
   or the new node-like sender.

@code
template <typename ExecOrGraph>
auto axpby(const ExecOrGraph& exec, ...)
{
    return exec | Kokkos::graph::parallel_for(
        Kokkos::RangePolicy<ExecOrGraph>(...),
        Functor{...}
    );
}
@endcode

There are drawbacks to this approach:
- The ETI matrix would probably explode, because you not only have to explicitly
  instantiate for the space instance types, but also for the type-erased sender-like
  node types (*e.g.* @c Kokkos::Experimental::GraphNodeRef<Kokkos::Cuda>).
- For big libraries, it incurs a lot of changes (basically each and every public API function
  but also probably internals).

Therefore, this approach could be a good match for new libraries,
but seems unrealistic for existing ones, especially that the rewritting needs to occurs
prior to users can effectively use @c Graph with the libraries.

@note In terms of impact on @c Kokkos core, this approach is very light. It only requires
      that we extend a bit the API on the @c Graph side, but it's not intrusive.

\subsection adoption-transparent Transparent adoption

A possibility could be to make a sort of type-erased space instance type, that at runtime
can either be a "regular" space instance or a @c Graph.

The idea is that we don't want to require some libraries to rewrite anything,
and make the @c Graph adoption **transparent** to them.

So we're back again with the same library code as if @c Graph never existed. Only the user
code explicitly asks for a @c Graph, and @c Kokkos does the rest under the hood.
This is somewhat inspired from
https://github.com/kokkos/kokkos/blob/b43f7dfbd0050fe30b12bccc816de8a48bc27074/core/unit_test/hpx/TestHPX_IndependentInstances.cpp#L90-L133.

@code
template <typename Exec, typename...>
void axpby(const Exec& exec, ...)
{
    Kokkos::parallel_for(
        Kokkos::RangePolicy<Exec>(exec, ...),
        Functor{...}
    );
}

template <typename Exec, typename...>
void Kokkos::parallel_for(const Exec& exec, ...) {
    if(exec.is_sender()) {
        // Append a new node to the graph, no execution yet.
    } else {
        // Regular @c Kokkos::parallel_for with an execution space instance and no graph.
    }
}
@endcode

There are pros and cons to this approach.

The obvious good point is that this approach does not require any change in existing
(big) libraries: it is transparent to them.

There are drawbacks:
1. Since we use type-erasure, we prevent any compile-time optimization (if any).
2. The code for @c Kokkos::parallel_for gets much longer (bigger files, longer compiler time),
   even if the end users of these libraries never use the @c Graph feature.

@note Compared to the approach described in @ref adoption-rewrite-api, this one is much more
      intrusice into the @c Kokkos core source code.

\section reminder Why use @c Graph while still keeping compatibility with "regular" execution space instances ?

\subsection runtime-overhead Creating a @c Graph adds overheads that need to be amortized

Creating a @c Graph induces some runtime overhead.

This overhead is amortized by re-submitting the graph many times.

But if your workflow runs only once, creating a @c Graph might not be an option,
especially if the workloads are small.

\subsection resusable-elementary-blocks Building blocks are usable in many contexts

Say you are responsible for a @c BLAS library.

Some functions are very tiny building blocks, *e.g.* @c dot. These tiny building blocks can be
expected to be used in many different user codes, and for sure some of these codes will just
want "regular" execution space instance behavior while others will want to incorporate
these blocks in their @c Graph.

\section limitations-for-adoption Limitations for broad adoption

Several limitations have been encountered already:
1. The size of a memory node's allocation is fixed, and must be known prior to graph definition.
   It cannot be updated (there is an API call @c cudaGraphMemAllocNodeGetParams but currently no corresponding
   API call to set the parameters of a memory node after graph definition).
   See @ref native/test_memory_node.cpp.
2. Host nodes cannot make @c Cuda API calls. See @ref native/test_host_node.cpp.
3. Representing an entire workflow as a single graph is not always possible. 
   If certain steps cannot be represented as device or host nodes, the workflow must be split into several graphs.

**/
